  <p>If you switch programs every couple of weeks, you aren't really following a program at all. Strength coach Jordan Hoppel explains how long to commit and why.</p>

  <h2>The short answer: 8 to 12 weeks</h2>
  <p>For a first program, Jordan wants people to give it 8 to 12 weeks. The reason is how a good program is built.</p>

  <h2>Why week one tells you almost nothing</h2>
  <p>In a periodized program, reps, sets, and exercises change from the first week to the last, and they change for a reason. If you only do week one of every program, you never see how it is designed to progress.</p>

  <Media type="image" alt="Training log showing weights rising across an 8 to 12 week block" caption="A training log across a full block shows what week one never can." brief="Screenshot of a client's training log across 8-12 weeks (app or spreadsheet), with the progression visible." />

  <h2>Why early gains feel fast, and then don't</h2>
  <p>The body needs time for tissues to adapt. Early on, most progress is neurological, not muscular, although people often credit the muscle. Because of that, it takes longer for changes to take hold in the muscle itself.</p>

  <JordanQuote>Once training age is higher and training is consistent, readjustment after time off is much quicker. The rebound is really quick.</JordanQuote>

  <h2>What to tell yourself when you want to switch</h2>
  <p>Finish the block first. Judge the program on how it performs across all 8 to 12 weeks, not on how week one felt. If you're unsure whether the program fits you in the first place, start with <a href="/how-to-choose-a-workout-program/">how to choose a workout program</a>.</p>
</GuideLayout>
